From WILSON_LJ@a1.tcc.corning.com Tue Dec  5 17:43:51 1995 CST
Message-ID: <01HYGIGJ1OIU9YCT8K@mr.tcc.corning.com>
Date: Tue, 05 Dec 1995 17:17:00 -0500 (EST)
From: Lillie J. Wilson 4-5204 <WILSON_LJ@a1.tcc.corning.com>
Subject: Cobra vs Alphaserver 2000

Hi All,
    
    I would like to get some feedback concerning purchasing of a new 
    system.  Presently in my shop we have a DEC 5000/200 (Ultrix v4.4) 
    running a print and NFS server for 5 DEC 3000/600 (Digital Unix 3.2)  
    and another DEC 5000/200.  Also, only seven people log on directly to 
    the DEC 5000 for TPU editor, file restores, and to use decnet for 
    "dlogin" to the WAN.  Six of the seven are the DEC 3000/600 users.
    
    The DEC 5000/200 is also a Pathworks server for 40 networked Pathworks 
    (V4.1) PCs and each PC usually has at 7 - 10 server connections for 
    various file services.  We heavily use FOXPRO as our database software 
    and also hanging off the DEC5000 is a raid disk drive system.  MSOFFICE 
    and most all PC applications are installed on the DEC 5000 disk drives 
    and we have several HP laser printers, one high-end plotter, and one 
    low-end plotter.  There aren't many locally installed PC applications.
    
    My shop were looking at purchasing an Alphaserver 2000 to replace our 
    aging DEC 5000.  Our main concerns is growth potential (additional 
    users, databases, etc.), technology (don't want an obsolete system in 
    two years), and disk access (because of the number of databases).  We 
    would also like clustering because the entire shop stops if the one 
    system goes down.
    
    We are now considering the Alphaserver 2100 because of technology 
    concerns.  The shop is very gun-shy over this because they purchased 
    the DEC 3000/600 over the past two years old and they will not be able 
    to go NT on them.  On the otherhand, a salesperson is recommending a 
    Cobra system instead of an ALphaServer because it can run Ultrix now 
    for us, can be a NT server next year, and it doesn't cost as much as an 
    AlphaServer.
    
    Please look over my shoulder and let me know what questions I should be 
    asking, which direction is the better route (Cobra vs. AlphaServer), 
    and if there any glaring changes that should be made.  I been working 
    here for four months and because of this list I will be taking a look 
    at SAMBA.
    
    Thanks!!! 
    
    I promise to summarize!!! 
    
    WILSON_LJ@CORNING.COM

