From scott@abyss.psc.edu Wed Dec  6 22:23:29 1995 CST
Message-ID: <9512070422.AA03800@abyss.psc.edu>
Date: Wed, 06 Dec 95 23:22:09 -0500
From: J. Ray Scott <scott@abyss.psc.edu>
Subject: AXP NT woes

I share the sentiments voiced in several recent messages that non-Intel NT
is a very difficult road to travel.  (My thanks to the several people who
helped me get a working Netscape on my AXP NT box.  This list is great.)

While many programs do work, it is still a crap-shoot everytime I get a new
package.  What also concerns me is the number of new, very popular packages
(Mosaic, Kermit) whose authors have advertised on their web pages that they
aren't porting to RISC NT.  In at least one case it was a lack of hardware.

The emulator and the translator are not the answer.  The emulator is slow and
has its limitations (protected mode, etc.)  I have a great deal of experience
with the mx translator and some with the Solaris translator.  These are
brilliant pieces of work but are very VERY difficult to manage.  You have
to have just the right version of a) the translator, b) the source-OS,
c) the target-OS and d) your application.  Let any change or, worse, let
there be a bug, and you're in deep trouble.  The translator can work as a
stopgap but should not be viewed as a solution.  The promise of NT was that
its architecture made porting applications across hardware boundaries very
easy so all apps would be available !native! on all hardware platforms.
This promise is not coming true.

The members of this list are gaining an experience base that ought to be of
interest to DEC.  I'm wondering if it is worth gathering the experiences, perhaps
organized by software package, so that others may benefit.  Several DEC
people seem to watch this list.  Perhaps it would help motivate them to help.

My concern is that if things don't change fast, RISC NT will die.  The list of
apps that was pointed out from the DEC ftp site is from July.  That's a age
in this business.

I have no concrete suggestion for organizing the experiences, excpet perhaps
to put a hook off the AlphaNT page from garply, hence adding an additional
burden to the already busy Aaron.  Perhaps there's a way to share the page
maintenance load?  

Thoughts?  I suppose another idea is just for each of us to go to the nearest
DEC office and let the air out of all Taurus tires.

	J. Ray Scott

	Technical Facilites Manager
	Pitsburgh Supercomputing Center

