From chris@flightinc.com Thu Dec  7 10:59:12 1995 CST
Message-ID: <199512071655.AA29266@mail.tyrell.net>
Date: Thu, 7 Dec 1995 10:55:50 -0600
From: Chris Silva <chris@flightinc.com>
Subject: Re: AXP NT woes

As a Digital Semiconductor OEM selling Alpha/NT boxes, I share many of your
frustrations. I have been involved in RISC NT since Oct. 1989, and have
ridden the MIPS and now Alpha waves in an attempt to carve a niche with
specialty machines.

The biggest holdup on gaining broad application availability, even in niche
areas (such as 3D) is the lack of development hardware. Many developers,
both small and large, either will not invest capital to support a
"questionable" platform, or cannot obtain loaner/free hardware to complete
the cross compiles.

If DEC wants to gain footholds, they must be willing to spend real dollars
in creating low-cost seed development machines. The Multia is a great start
in this direction. If DEC would cut their media advertising budget by a
couple million, and invest that money in developer seed machines, you would
see a steady growth of applications. 

Many of my customers (and I would bet readers of this list) use an Alpha
machine to accomplish a specific, time-intensive task. Unfortunately, the
dearth of applications forces the system to be used for only that task,
while Pentiums abound for running the general office functions. This stops
the Alpha evangelism cold.

If DEC wants to break the Intel stranglehold, they must focus on COMPLETE
penetration of specific markets, until the user base becomes large enough to
force developers to create Alpha versions or lose sales. I see that DEC is
taking that approach is some areas, such as Internet servers, and it is
working, but more developer assistance and development hardware needs to be
made available to gather up a widening base of applications. 

Pick the markets where time is money, buy the key applications, then seed
the rest to assure a complete base of applications to service that area.
Eventually others will be forced to come along.

Just my two cents...

Regards,
Chris Silva
Flight Technologies, Inc.
(816) 525-UFLY (8359)
Unparalleled Windows NT Workstation Solutions

