From paul@beicorp.com Thu Dec 14 16:22:20 1995 CST
Message-ID: <19951214222127776.AAA47@holly>
Date: Thu, 14 Dec 1995 14:21:28 -0800
From: Paul Bunn <paul@beicorp.com>
Subject: Visual C++ 4.0 for AXP

Visual C++ 4.0 has to be one of _the_ best pieces of software yet devised by
mankind, and I am very fortunate to be able to use it.  However, let
everyone be aware that there currently exists a serious bug in the optimizer
which can generate bad code that crashes your application when run.  I found
that my debug version of UltraBac worked flawlessly, yet when I compiled it
for release it crashed.  After many hours at looking at compiler-generated
assembler, I tracked down the offending statement to be a simple assignment
in a straight-forward routine.  Even disabling all optimizations for this
entire routine do not make amends.  Needless to say, all optimizations have
been disabled in the currently shipping AXP versions of UltraBac.  The x86
platform does not exhibit this behavior (identical code set).  Microsoft are
working hard with me to establish a solution to the problem -- I will make
another post when I have further information.

Regards

